<h2>A 2009 booking, a 95% payment, no flat</h2>
<p>The buyer booked an apartment in a Hyderabad project in 2009 for about ₹2.30 crore. He paid ₹51.11 lakh from his own funds to the builder and serviced a ₹2 crore HDFC loan — about ₹1.69 crore of which was disbursed to the builder in thirteen tranches, with the buyer paying ₹76.23 lakh towards the loan. The builder received nearly 95% of the consideration but never delivered possession in the promised period. In 2013 the buyer approached the National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission seeking refund with 18% interest, rent reimbursement and compensation. The NCDRC ordered a refund with 9% interest (12% on default), leaving rent and compensation undecided; both sides and the bank pursued appeals.</p>

<h2>The three rulings</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Own funds, not loan funds</h4><p>The buyer recovers ₹1,27,34,726 — the sums traceable to his own pocket. The loan disbursement is not refunded to him, since money borrowed and still unpaid &ldquo;does not become his own money&rdquo;; recovery of that component awaits the bank-builder accounts.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Contract rate of interest</h4><p>Interest runs at 12% per annum, simple, from the date of each payment — the rate the Agreement for Sale itself fixed in Clause 24. The demand for 18% failed.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>No rent atop interest</h4><p>Interest compensates the deprivation of money; rent for the same period over the same default is double compensation. The rent claim also failed on pleading and proof.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The final directions</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Item</th><th>Direction</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Refund to buyer</td><td>₹1,27,34,726 with 12% simple interest from the respective payment dates until payment</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Lump-sum compensation</td><td>₹15,00,000 from the builder, in full and final satisfaction of rent, mental agony, harassment and litigation expenses, releasable within one month</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Bank&rsquo;s deficiency</td><td>₹2,00,000 award against the bank upheld</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Loan component</td><td>Buyer&rsquo;s liability and the bank&rsquo;s recovery left to the bank&rsquo;s appeal before the High Court, to be decided independently; balance deposit retained in an interest-bearing account to abide by that outcome</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What this means for stalled-project litigation</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Map the money first.</strong> In tripartite-loan purchases, counsel must separate the buyer&rsquo;s own payments from bank disbursements at the pleading stage, because the refund decree will follow that line.</li>
    <li><strong>Plead rent properly or not at all.</strong> A rent claim raised late, without rent receipts or a lease, now faces both an evidentiary and a conceptual bar.</li>
    <li><strong>The contract rate anchors interest.</strong> Agreements that stipulate delay interest cut both ways — buyers get the stipulated rate, not an aspirational one.</li>
    <li><strong>Election has consequences.</strong> Choosing refund over possession forecloses possession-linked heads; the choice should be made deliberately, in writing, early.</li>
  </ul>
</div>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. Outcomes in consumer and real-estate disputes depend on the agreement and payment records in each case; the judgment should be read in full before being relied upon.</p></div>
